<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tayichiud</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Tayichiud were a Mongol clan closely related to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borjigin">Borjigin</a> and part of the Khamag Mongol confederation of the 12th century. After the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yesugei">Yesügei</a> they abandoned his widow and children and later held the young <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Temüjin</a>, the future Genghis Khan, captive until he escaped. Temüjin defeated them around 1200 and absorbed their people into his following.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Tayichiud were a subclan of the Kiyat Borjigin and one of the three core tribes of the Khamag Mongol confederation. They first appear in the record under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ambaghai">Ambaghai Khan</a> in 1148, but the clan was founded by his grandfather Chirhya Lynhua, son of Khaidu Khan, founder of the Kiyat clan. They lived in the south of present-day Zabaykalsky Krai and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a>'s Dornod Province. Though the Borjigids and the Tayichiud shared an ancestor in Bodonchar Munkhag, they were at times bitter rivals for the leadership of the Khamag Mongol.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khabul-khan">Khabul Khan</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borjigin">Borjigin</a> had seven sons but named <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ambaghai">Ambaghai</a>, son of Sengum Bilge of the Tayichiud, as his successor, and Ambaghai became the second khan of the Khamag Mongol. Rule then passed back and forth between the two lines until it came to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/secret-history-of-the-mongols">Secret History of the Mongols</a> portrays the Tayichiud as his bitter enemies, allied at times with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamukha">Jamukha</a>.</p><p>Genghis destroyed the ruling clan, but descendants who had surrendered rose high in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongol-empire">Mongol Empire</a>. Jebe, born Jurgaadai, of the Besud clan of the Tayichiud, struck the final blow to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jurchens">Jurchens</a> in the conquest of the Jin dynasty in 1219 and defeated the Kypchaks and their allies at the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalka">Kalka</a> in 1223. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baiju">Baiju</a>, commander of the Tammachi in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>, was also a Besud. Chilaun, one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a>'s four close companions, came from the Suldus subclan; his descendant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chupan">Chupan</a> was named chief commander of all Mongol khanates by the Yuan court in 1327. Buyan Suldus overthrew the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qaraunas">Qara'unas</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transoxiana">Transoxiana</a> in 1359 and was executed in 1362 by Tughluq Temur.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a> the tribe was led by Amir Musa, who clashed with Timur several times yet was bound to the dynasty by marriage. His daughter Tuman Agha and his niece Saray Mulk Khanum both married Timur, the latter becoming his chief consort, and his son Muhammad Beg married Timur's daughter Aka Begim. Their son Sultan Husayn Tayichiud held high posts in the army and was the last of the clan's line, ending in 1405. People with the clan name Taichiud or Taijiud are found today in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inner-mongolia">Inner Mongolia</a> and Kalmykia.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Taichiud” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The confederation was a loose alliance of Borjigin, <b>Tayichiud</b> and other clans, choosing khans at assemblies.</blockquote><a href="/state/khamag-mongol">Khamag Mongol</a></li></ul></section>
